" gray out the status bar(s) when this tmux pane loses focus (e.g. switching
" tmux panes - requires the tmux-side setup in dot_tmux.conf and
" dot_tmux-config/executable_sync-pane-focus.sh, which push FocusLost/
" FocusGained escape codes to whichever pane is running vim on every tmux
" pane switch; tmux's own `focus-events` only covers the outer terminal
" losing/gaining OS-level window focus, not an internal pane switch).
" ctermbg=NONE (the first version of this) left the cterm side unset, so
" nothing visibly changed here: this vimrc never sets 'termguicolors', so a
" plain terminal renders StatusLine from ctermbg, not guibg - the gui*
" fields alone were invisible.
"
" On FocusLost, StatusLineNC must match StatusLine exactly (not just get a
" separate dim treatment): with the tmux pane itself unfocused, no :split
" inside it is "more current" than another from the user's point of view, so
" showing two different grays (STATUS's dark gray vs StatusLineNC's default,
" lighter one) made the officially-inactive split look MORE highlighted than
" the active one - the opposite of the intent.
"
" Three-way highlight order, brightest to dimmest: (1) tmux pane focused AND
" vim split focused - the vivid green StatusLine below; (2) tmux pane focused
" but vim split unfocused - a dim, desaturated-green StatusLineNC; (3) tmux
" pane unfocused entirely - flat dark gray, dimmer than (2), on both groups,
" since neither split is "more current" once the pane itself isn't focused.
" This used to be inverted: FocusLost's ctermbg=238/guibg=#767676 was
" lighter than FocusGained's inactive-split gray, so switching tmux panes
" away made the status line MORE eye-catching than staying in the focused
" pane on an unfocused split - the opposite of the intended hierarchy.
"
" `:highlight` only touches the attributes you name - StatusLine's cterm=bold
" (set once by the colorscheme, never named in any override below) survives
" every FocusLost/FocusGained switch untouched. That leftover bold on
" ctermfg=black text over the FocusLost gray was rendering as invisible in
" iTerm (bold + explicit black is drawn in iTerm's "bright" black, i.e.
" nearly the same dark gray as the background) while StatusLineNC - never
" bold - stayed legible right next to it. cterm=NONE/gui=NONE here forces
" both groups to the same non-bold weight while unfocused, and the
" FocusGained lines re-assert cterm=bold/gui=bold explicitly (previously
" implicit/inherited) so this can't silently regress the same way again.
augroup focus_statusline
  autocmd!
  autocmd FocusLost * highlight StatusLine cterm=NONE gui=NONE ctermfg=black guifg=#ffffff ctermbg=234 guibg=#1c1c1c
        \ | highlight StatusLineNC cterm=NONE gui=NONE ctermfg=black guifg=#ffffff ctermbg=234 guibg=#1c1c1c
  autocmd FocusGained,VimEnter * highlight StatusLine cterm=bold gui=bold ctermfg=black guifg=#ffffff ctermbg=34 guibg=#1f8a4c
        \ | highlight StatusLineNC cterm=NONE gui=NONE ctermfg=250 guifg=#888888 ctermbg=22 guibg=#1a3524
augroup END
" VimEnter is included above because a vim spawned mid-pane (e.g. Claude
" Code's transcript viewer, Ctrl+O then v) starts as a new foreground
" process inside an already-active tmux pane - no pane-switch event fires,
" so dot_tmux-config/executable_sync-pane-focus.sh never runs and this vim
" never receives an explicit FocusGained. Without this, its StatusLine sat
" at colorscheme's raw default indefinitely. A vim you just launched into
" an active pane is virtually always focused, so applying the "focused"
" style at startup is a safe assumption.
" }}}
" }}}
" ideaVim ignore end

